findings.body:
Removes the `ALLOWED` origin allowlist and now echoes any request `Origin` (or `*` when absent) while always setting `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`.

**Failure mode:** A page on an attacker origin can issue a credentialed cross-origin request to this API. The browser sends cookies/session material, and because `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` mirrors the attacker origin, JavaScript on that page can read the response body — full cross-site data theft for any cookie-authenticated endpoint behind this middleware.

Native mobile clients are outside browser CORS and do not need this; webviews that do still require an explicit allowlist of trusted origins.

**Fix:** Keep credentials only for known origins, e.g. restore a set membership check (and extend the set with real mobile-webview origins if needed). Never pair `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` with credentials.

```suggestion
  const ALLOWED = new Set([
    "https://app.example.com",
    // add known mobile webview origins explicitly
  ]);
  if (origin && ALLOWED.has(origin)) {
    return {
      "Access-Control-Allow-Origin": origin,
      "Access-Control-Allow-Credentials": "true",
      Vary: "Origin",
    };
  }
  return { Vary: "Origin" };
```
